#: ../folio-assistant-core/processes/library/ingest-extract-structure.bpmn:83
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "Contextual retrieval: a chunk in isolation loses what makes it mean anything, so each section carries the document brief. An outline is set aside when its entries are machine bookmark names (_GoBack, _Hlk..., \"Blank Page\", a merged file's name) or when under half of them name a heading the pages print, and the contents are inferred instead (diagnostics.outline_rejected). A section that would hold more than half of a document of 20 pages or more (and over 8,000 words), or over 20,000 words anywhere, is cut at the headings inferred inside it, else one section per page (diagnostics.oversized_split). Owner, 2026-11-11: \"split single blocks that hold a whole book\". Method: skill l1-document-ingestion, \"An outline that is not contents, and a section too long to be a block\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../folio-assistant-core/processes/library/ingest-extract-structure.bpmn:51
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "Does the binary carry an embedded text layer? `yes` extracts it; `no — scanned` runs OCR into ocr/page-*.txt."
msgstr ""

#: ../folio-assistant-core/processes/library/ingest-extract-structure.bpmn:74
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "Each page is read in the document's languages (--lang-hint, from dc.language.iso), narrowed to the script the page shows. A page Tesseract is unsure of (mean word confidence under 70) is read again with each other installed script added, and the most confident read is kept: a French article's Arabic abstract, or the Latin URLs in an Arabic document's references. A document ingested this way keeps its text in ocr/ and only a stub in sections/, which the documented sections/ grep cannot see. That asymmetry is why the corpus-grep checklist has a fourth tier. The run also records WHICH SOFTWARE made the pages, in ocr/run-context.json: a folio-run-context/v1 (cat-harness schemas/run-context.ts, the record test runs and QA reports use for the same question), naming the OCR engine, the rasteriser and each language model with its version and the sha256 of the bytes that ran. Two OCRs of one scan can read differently (\"Manual\" and \"Manval\"), and without it neither could be told from the other or reproduced."
msgstr ""

